#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# dot

#% text
# `dot(a, b)` gives the product of the vectors or matrices `a` and `b`.

#% text
# `dot(a, b, c, ...)` multiplies from the left: `dot(dot(a, b), c)`, and so on.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# A vector is a list and a matrix a list of rows. Two vectors give the scalar product; a matrix and a vector give a vector; two matrices give the matrix product.

#% item
# A vector on the left multiplies like a row: `dot(v, m)` is the vector of the sums `v[i]*m[i, j]`.

#% item
# Complex entries are not conjugated: `dot(u, v)` is the sum of `u[i]*v[i]`.

#% item
# The inner sizes must agree; otherwise the result is `failure(shape_mismatch, dot)`.

#% item
# `dot(a)` is `a`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The scalar product of two vectors:

#% cell
dot([1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6])
#% output
#= 32

#% text
# A matrix times a vector:

#% cell
dot([[1, 2], [3, 4]], [5, 6])
#% output
#= [17, 39]

#% text
# The product of two matrices:

#% cell
dot([[1, 2], [3, 4]], [[0, 1], [1, 0]])
#% output
#= [[2, 1], [4, 3]]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Symbolic entries:

#% cell
dot([a, b], [c, d])
#% output
#= a*c + b*d

#% text
# A vector on the left:

#% cell
dot([5, 6], [[1, 2], [3, 4]])
#% output
#= [23, 34]

#% text
# Several factors:

#% cell
dot([[1, 2], [3, 4]], [[5, 6], [7, 8]], [1, 1])
#% output
#= [41, 93]

#% text
# A row times a column, and a column times a row:

#% cell
dot([[1, 2, 3]], [[1], [2], [3]])
#% output
#= [[14]]

#% cell
dot([[1], [2], [3]], [[1, 2, 3]])
#% output
#= [[1, 2, 3], [2, 4, 6], [3, 6, 9]]

#% text
# Balls:

#% cell
dot(n([1, pi], 20), [1, 1])
#% output
#= 4.14159265358979323846264

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Fibonacci numbers from powers of a matrix:

#% cell
m = [[1, 1], [1, 0]]; dot(m, m, m, m, m)
#% output
#= [[8, 5], [5, 3]]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The identity matrix leaves a vector unchanged:

#% cell
dot(identity_matrix(2), [x, y])
#% output
#= [x, y]

#% text
# The Euclidean norm is the square root of the scalar product with itself:

#% cell
u = [1, 2, 2]; sqrt(dot(u, u)) == norm(u)
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Without conjugation, a nonzero complex vector can have scalar product 0 with itself:

#% cell
dot([1, im], [1, im])
#% output
#= 0

#% cell
dot([1, im], conjugate([1, im]))
#% output
#= 2

#% text
# Vectors of different lengths:

#% cell
dot([1, 2], [1, 2, 3])
#% output
#! failure(shape_mismatch, dot)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `inner`, `outer`, `cross`, `norm`, `transpose`, `kronecker_product`
